[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A whole-project export reads every .sgra under the root, referenced or not

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SGRA-SCANNED-PROJECT-WIDE
TITLE: A whole-project export reads every .sgra under the root, referenced or not
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc export . reads every file with an .sgra extension anywhere beneath the
project root, whether or not a document imports it. A grammar file that fails to
parse therefore fails the whole export, from wherever it sits in the tree.

Measured on 0.28.3 while committing the negative fixture corpus for the grammar
milestone: six deliberately invalid grammars under
packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/negative/ took the repository-wide export
from exit 0 to exit 1, on a file nothing referenced. They now carry an
.sgra.invalid suffix so the scan skips them, which works but encodes the
constraint in a filename rather than in configuration.

exclude_doc_paths in strictdoc_config.py is the configured answer and already
carries two globs for the same class of problem with .md files. Whether the
fixture suffix or a third glob is the right shape is undecided; the glob keeps
fixtures under their natural extension, the suffix keeps the config from
listing every directory that might hold one.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure is remote from its cause -- the export names the fixture, but
nothing connects "a file I added under packages/" to "the command that reads the
design graph". A session that adds an invalid grammar anywhere and then sees the
graph command break has no reason to look in packages/ for it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
A second producer of .sgra files under the root arrived 2026-08-27: the
ai.strictdoc module's generate:sgra task writes every declared grammar into the
working tree. It MITIGATES this node rather than resolving it. The task writes
through a temporary file carrying a LEADING DOT, so a crashed run cannot strand
a visible `grammar.sgra.XXXXXX` beside the real one for a whole-project export
to pick up as another grammar.

That is the constraint encoded in a filename for a second time, next to the
fixtures' .sgra.invalid suffix. Two encodings of one rule, neither of them in
strictdoc_config.py, is the argument for settling the exclude_doc_paths question
rather than an argument against it.
<<<
